[{"id":"29621","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">As per the Industrial Policy Resolution 1948, which of the following sector was reserved for public sector only?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Ship building</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Telephone</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Industrial Policy Resolution (IPR) 1948 laid down four categories of industries. The first category — comprising arms and ammunition, atomic energy, and railways — was reserved exclusively for the Central Government. Ship building and telephone (telecommunications) equipment manufacturing fell in the second category, in which new undertakings could ordinarily be set up only by the State, though existing private units were allowed to continue and expand for 10 years. Since both these industries were effectively reserved for the public sector under IPR 1948, the correct answer is Both I and II.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29622","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The Satavahana kings were succeeded by the rulers of which dynasty?</span></p>","option_1":"Maurya dynasty","option_2":"Nanda dynasty","option_3":"Ikshvaku dynasty","option_4":"Shishunaga dynasty","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Ikshvaku dynasty.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Satavahanas ruled over the Deccan region until around the early 3rd century CE. After their decline, the Ikshvaku dynasty (also called the Andhra Ikshvakus) rose to power in the Krishna-Guntur region of Andhra Pradesh, with Vijayapuri (Nagarjunakonda) as their capital. The Maurya and Nanda dynasties preceded the Satavahanas chronologically, and the Shishunaga dynasty was an even earlier Magadhan dynasty, so neither fits as a 'successor'.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29623","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following articles of the Indian Constitution provides Provisions as to introduction and passing of Bills in parliament?</span></p>","option_1":"Article 101","option_2":"Article 105","option_3":"Article 107","option_4":"Article 103","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Article 107.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Article 107 of the Constitution deals with 'Provisions as to introduction and passing of Bills', stating that a Bill (other than a Money Bill or Constitution Amendment Bill) may originate in either House of Parliament and must be passed by both Houses before it goes for the President's assent. Article 101 deals with vacation of seats, Article 105 with powers and privileges of Parliament and its members, and Article 103 with disqualification of MPs on grounds decided by the President.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29624","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">At which Harappan site was a dockyard found?</span></p>","option_1":"Banawali","option_2":"Lothal","option_3":"Rakhigarhi","option_4":"Kalibangan","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Lothal.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Lothal, located in Gujarat on the Sabarmati basin, is famous for the remains of a man-made dockyard, indicating its importance as a port town and centre of maritime trade during the Harappan (Indus Valley) Civilization. Banawali and Kalibangan (both in present-day India) are known for fire altars and a ploughed field, respectively, while Rakhigarhi is one of the largest Harappan sites but is not known for a dockyard.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29625","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which direction do roots of a plant typically grow in response to light?</span></p>","option_1":"Towards light","option_2":"Away from light","option_3":"Always parallel to the ground","option_4":"Away from gravity","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Away from light.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This phenomenon is called negative phototropism. While shoots exhibit positive phototropism (growing towards light to maximise photosynthesis), roots generally grow away from light and into the soil (negative phototropism), which also aligns with their positive geotropism/gravitropism (growth towards gravity, i.e., downward).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29626","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The growth rate of the Christian population in India between 2001 and 2011 was ______.</span></p>","option_1":"15.5%","option_2":"21.5%","option_3":"10.5%","option_4":"19.5%","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 15.5%.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per Census 2011 data, the Christian population of India grew at a rate of about 15.5% during 2001-2011, slightly lower than the overall national population growth rate of around 17.7% for the same decade. This is a factual figure frequently asked in SSC General Awareness sections from Census data.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29627","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">What component inside the spring balance stretches when a force is applied?</span></p>","option_1":"Coiled spring","option_2":"A clock","option_3":"Metal rod","option_4":"Lever","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Coiled spring.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A spring balance works on Hooke's Law, wherein the coiled spring inside it stretches (elongates) in proportion to the force/weight applied on the hook. The extension of the spring is calibrated on a scale to directly read off the weight or force. A clock, metal rod, and lever are not the elastic sensing elements used in a spring balance.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29628","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Veda Samaj, established in Chennai, was inspired by which of the following?</span></p>","option_1":"Ramakrishna Mission","option_2":"Prarthana Samaj","option_3":"Arya Samaj","option_4":"Brahmo Samaj","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Brahmo Samaj.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Veda Samaj was founded in Madras (Chennai) in 1864 by Keshub Chandra Sen along with local reformers, and it was directly inspired by the Brahmo Samaj's ideals of monotheism and social reform, later being renamed the South Indian Brahmo Samaj. It was not linked to the Ramakrishna Mission, Prarthana Samaj (a Maharashtra-based reform movement), or the Arya Samaj (founded by Swami Dayanand Saraswati).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29629","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which musical instrument is shown in the figure given below? (A long-necked bowed instrument with strings and a bow resting beside it)</span></p>","option_1":"Pakhawaj","option_2":"Jhanj","option_3":"Algoza","option_4":"Sindhi Sarangi","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Sindhi Sarangi.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Sindhi Sarangi is a bowed, short-necked string (chordophone) instrument traditionally used to accompany folk singers, particularly in Sindh and Rajasthan. Pakhawaj is a two-headed membranophone (percussion) instrument, Jhanj is a pair of cymbals (idiophone), and Algoza is a wind (aerophone) double-flute instrument — none of which match a bowed string instrument shown in such figures.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29630","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In which of the following years did Gandhiji go to London for the Second Round Table Conference?</span></p>","option_1":"1930","option_2":"1931","option_3":"1932","option_4":"1933","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 1931.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Mahatma Gandhi attended the Second Round Table Conference held in London from September to December 1931, representing the Indian National Congress following the Gandhi-Irwin Pact of March 1931. The First Round Table Conference (1930) and the Third (1932) were held without Congress participation.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29631","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The Kodaikanal Solar Observatory (KSO), which completed 125 years in 2024, is located in which Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Kerala","option_2":"Telangana","option_3":"Tamil Nadu","option_4":"Maharashtra","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Tamil Nadu.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Kodaikanal Solar Observatory, operated by the Indian Institute of Astrophysics, is located in Kodaikanal, Tamil Nadu, and completed 125 years of solar observations in 2024 (it was established in 1899). It is one of the oldest solar observatories in Asia and holds continuous digitised sunspot records dating back over a century.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29632","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In Lok Sabha, how many seats are reserved for Scheduled Castes?</span></p>","option_1":"84","option_2":"54","option_3":"56","option_4":"92","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 84.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per the current delimitation, 84 seats in the Lok Sabha are reserved for Scheduled Castes (SC) and 47 for Scheduled Tribes (ST), out of the total 543 seats, under Articles 330 and 332 of the Constitution which provide for reservation of seats in proportion to their population.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29633","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">What technique uses ultrasonic waves to form images of the heart?</span></p>","option_1":"Electrocardiography","option_2":"Sonography","option_3":"Echocardiography","option_4":"Radiography","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Echocardiography.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Echocardiography specifically uses ultrasonic (high-frequency sound) waves to produce images of the heart's chambers, valves, and blood flow. Electrocardiography (ECG) records the heart's electrical activity, general sonography is a broader ultrasound imaging term for other body parts, and radiography uses X-rays rather than sound waves.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29634","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">As per National Mineral Inventory on 01.04.2020, which Indian State hosts the country's 90% diamond?</span></p>","option_1":"Madhya Pradesh","option_2":"Gujarat","option_3":"Andhra Pradesh","option_4":"Assam","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Madhya Pradesh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>According to the National Mineral Inventory as on 01.04.2020, Madhya Pradesh (particularly the Panna belt) accounts for around 90% of India's total diamond reserves/resources, making it the leading diamond-bearing state in the country, followed distantly by Chhattisgarh and Andhra Pradesh.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29635","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"770","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-si